  "generated_text": "1. We know that the altitudes divide the sides into segments of lengths $5, 3, 2,$ and $x$ units.\n2. The sum of the lengths of these segments must equal the length of the third side of the triangle.\n3. Using the Pythagorean theorem, we can find the length of the third side.\n\nLet's denote the length of the third side as $c$.\n\n4. Applying the Pythagorean theorem to the right triangle formed by the altitudes:\n\\[\nc^2 = 5^2 + 3^2 + 2^2\n\\]\n\\[\nc^2 = 25 + 9 + 4\n\\]\n\\[\nc^2 = 38\n\\]\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{38}\n\\]\n\n5. Summing the lengths of the segments:\n\\[\n5 + 3 + 2 + x = \\sqrt{36}\n\\]\n\\[\n5 + x = 6\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 6 - 5\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[1, '1']",
    "gold_answer": "Let us label this diagram. [asy]\ndefaultpen(linewidth(0.7)); size(120);\npair A = (0,0);\npair B = (1,0);\npair C = (74/136,119/136);\npair D = foot(B, A, C);\npair E = /*foot(A, B, C)*/ (52*B+(119-52)*C)/(119);\ndraw(A--B--C--cycle);\ndraw(B--D);\ndraw(A--E);\ndraw(rightanglemark(A,D,B,1.2));\ndraw(rightanglemark(A,E,B,1.2));\nlabel(\"$A$\", A, S);\nlabel(\"$B$\", B, S);\nlabel(\"$C$\", C, N);\nlabel(\"$D$\", D, NW);\nlabel(\"$E$\", E, NE);\nlabel(\"$3$\",(C+D)/2,WNW+(0,0.3));\nlabel(\"$5$\",(A+D)/2,NW);\nlabel(\"$2$\",(C+E)/2,E);\nlabel(\"$x$\",(B+E)/2,NE);\n[/asy] $\\triangle ACE$ and $\\triangle BCD$ are similar by AA since they share $\\angle ACB$ and $\\angle AEC$ and $\\angle BDC$ are both right angles and hence congruent.  So $$\\frac{CE}{CD} = \\frac{AC}{BC}.$$ Plugging in values, we have $$\\frac23 = \\frac{8}{x+2}.$$ Solving this gives $x+2 = 12,$ or $x = \\boxed{10}.$",
    "is_correct": false
  },
